// The Heligoland Bight, 28 August 1914 at 06:50 GMT: Tyrwhitt's Harwich Force sweeps south-east into the Bight in a
// morning mist to fall on the German destroyer patrols north-west of Heligoland; Goodenough's light cruisers follow, and
// Beatty's battlecruisers wait far to the north-west, unknown to the destroyers' commodores. The German light cruisers
// come out piecemeal from the Jade, the Elbe and the Ems to the sound of the guns, into the mist, one by one; the
// battlecruisers of the High Seas Fleet can't cross the Jade bar until the afternoon's tide. History's forces, less the
// submarines and Cruiser Force C far to the west; the classes are stand-ins (l_laforey for the L and I classes,
// scout_cruiser for Fearless, the towns for the 1st LCS; v1 for the German boats, stettin for Stettin, Cöln and Mainz,
// gazelle for Frauenlob, Ariadne and Danzig, graudenz for Strassburg and Stralsund):
//   Britain, the trap: thirty-one destroyers and eight cruisers in the mist, and five battlecruisers behind them.
//   Germany, the defence: patrols and cruisers near home, each alone until the others come up; 105 mm guns and
//   good shooting against the destroyers, and nothing that can stand up to a battlecruiser.
// Beatty covers the light cruisers from 45 km (a cover task holds him to them, as he waited in history) until his
// commander sends him in. A British scenario, as history was: the AI loses ten British ships a run and nearly every
// German one (4 seeds); the challenge is the cost. Hipper's battlecruisers out over the bar in time were tried, and
// only made it bloodier both ways; the light ships' fragility is in docs/battle/bugs.md.
// The weather of the morning: mist, visibility about 5 km, calm.
